README

This sample program turns a Raspberry Pi with two Ethernet ports into an
EtherCAT master for Elmo servo drives, with a web interface, which shows the
drive parameters and status, and allows to control the drives from a browser.

	+-------------------+  eth0 (EtherCAT)    +--------------+
	|  Raspberry Pi     |-------------------->| Elmo drive(s)|
	|  Circle+SOEM+JSD  |                     +--------------+
	|                   |  eth1 (TCP/IP)
	|                   |--------------------->  your browser
	+-------------------+

The drives are controlled with JSD (JPL Servo Drive) by the Jet Propulsion
Laboratory, which is used through its Circle port in addon/jsd. Both Elmo
drive families are supported: Gold drives (jsd_egd) and Platinum drives in
nominal mode (jsd_epd_nominal). The drives are detected automatically from
their EtherCAT product code.

	!!! THIS PROGRAM MOVES MOTORS FROM A WEB PAGE. READ THE SAFETY  !!!
	!!! SECTION BELOW BEFORE YOU CONNECT A DRIVE TO A MACHINE.      !!!

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5)
	* One or more Elmo Gold or Platinum drives on the on-board port
	* A USB Ethernet device with a CDC Ethernet (ECM) interface for the
	  web interface, connected to a network with a DHCP server. On the
	  Raspberry Pi 5 it has to be attached to a root port directly,
	  because USB 3 hubs are not supported.


BUILD

Download and build SOEM and JSD first:

	cd addon/soem
	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git
	make

	cd ../jsd
	git clone https://github.com/nasa-jpl/jsd.git
	make

Then build this sample:

	cd sample/48-elmoweb
	make

If libjsd.a has not been built, the build of this sample is skipped with a
message, so that a build of all samples does not fail.


BEFORE YOU START: DRIVE PARAMETERS

When the bus goes operational, JSD writes the drive parameters from its
configuration into the drives. The values are defined at the top of
elmodrive.h and are intentionally very conservative:

	ELMO_PEAK_CURRENT_A		1.0 A	(PL[1])
	ELMO_CONTINUOUS_CURRENT_A	0.5 A	(CL[1])
	ELMO_MAX_MOTOR_SPEED		100000 counts/s
	ELMO_LOW/HIGH_POSITION_LIMIT	0	(position limits disabled)

ADAPT THESE VALUES TO YOUR MOTOR before you connect one. A current limit,
which is too high for your motor, can damage it. The drive rejects the
configuration, if the continuous current is above the peak current, or if the
peak current is above the maximum current of the drive (MC[1]) - that check is
left active on purpose.

The parameter CRC check of JSD is overridden (crc = INT32_MIN), because the
CRC of your drive is not known to this sample. In a real application you would
put the CRC of your commissioned drive there, so that JSD refuses to run, if
somebody changed the drive parameters.

The commands, which come in over the web interface, are clamped in the master
against these limits (see StartMotion() in elmodrive.cpp):

	ELMO_MAX_JOG_VELOCITY		20000 counts/s
	ELMO_MAX_MOVE_VELOCITY		20000 counts/s
	ELMO_MAX_TORQUE_A		the continuous current limit

The limits, which are displayed in the web interface, are informational. The
clamping in the master is what protects the machine.


WHAT IT DOES

* The on-board Ethernet device (net device 0) is used for EtherCAT. The bus is
  scanned first to find the Elmo drives and their families, then JSD is
  configured accordingly and brings the bus to the OPERATIONAL state.
* The process data is exchanged every 2 ms (ETHERCAT_CYCLE_TIME_US in
  kernel.h). The cycle uses an absolute deadline, so that the period does not
  drift, and counts the cycles, which were late ("missed deadlines" on the web
  page). The cycle time is written to the drives as the expected loop period
  (object 0x60C2), so both values must match.
* The second Ethernet device (net device 1) runs the TCP/IP stack with the web
  server on port 80. The IP address is written to the system log.

The web page shows per drive: the CiA 402 state machine state, the mode of
operation, the fault and EMCY code, the servo/motor/motion/STO/warning flags,
the actual and commanded position, velocity, current, bus voltage, drive
temperature and the digital inputs. The bus card shows the master state, the
link, the working counter, the cycle statistics and the jitter. The system card
shows the Raspberry Pi status.

The "Bus scan" card lists every slave, which was found on the bus, with the
identity from its EEPROM (name, vendor ID, product code, revision, serial) and,
if it has a CoE mailbox, the standard identification objects 0x1008 (device
name), 0x1009 (hardware version) and 0x100A (software version). The last column
says whether the slave is driven by this application, and if not, why. The same
information is written to the system log during the bus scan. Use this card to
identify a device, which this application does not drive.


CONTROL INTERFACE AND INTERLOCKS

Each drive has these controls:

	Enable		bring the drive to OPERATION ENABLED (repeated reset,
			which is how a drive is enabled with JSD)
	Disable		disable the drive
	Reset faults	acknowledge the faults and enable again
	Arm/Disarm	allow or forbid motion commands
	STOP		quick stop and disarm, always accepted
	Jog		velocity move, +/- the entered velocity
	Move to/by	absolute or relative position move
	Apply torque	torque command, with an extra confirmation

Four interlocks protect against the obvious accidents:

1. A motion command is rejected, unless the drive is enabled AND armed. The
   web page disables the motion buttons, but the master enforces it.
2. The master halts an armed drive and disarms it, if it does not see a request
   from the web interface within 500 ms (ELMO_WATCHDOG_MS). This covers a
   closed browser tab, a crashed browser and a pulled network cable during a
   jog. The web page polls the status every 200 ms, while a drive is armed.
3. All targets are clamped in the master, see above.
4. Torque mode asks for a confirmation in the browser, because it has no
   position or velocity limiting in the drive.

Note that the drives have their own protection against a lost master: JSD
configures 5 extrapolation cycles (object 0x2F75 on Gold, 0x3675 on Platinum)
and the quick stop ramp (object 0x605A = 2), so a drive stops by itself, if the
process data stops arriving.

	THERE IS NO AUTHENTICATION. Anybody, who can reach the web port, can
	move your motors. Put the web port on an isolated network.


COMMAND MODES

An Elmo Gold drive accepts either the profiled commands (the drive generates
the motion profile) or the cyclic synchronous commands (the master sends a
setpoint in each cycle), which is fixed, when the bus is configured. A Platinum
drive accepts both.

This sample configures the profiled mode by default (pDrive->Mode in
CElmoMaster::Discover()), because the drive generates the profile itself then,
which is much less sensitive to a jittery cycle time. If you switch a drive to
ElmoCommandModeCyclic, the master generates a trapezoidal profile in
UpdateCyclicMotion() and sends CSP/CSV/CST setpoints instead. Watch the "missed
deadlines" counter on the web page in this case.


TROUBLESHOOTING

The debug messages go to the serial interface, on the Raspberry Pi 5 to the
dedicated 3-pin UART connector (uart10) at 115200 baud, like in
sample/47-ethercatweb. Use "logtarget=tty1" in cmdline.txt to write them to the
screen instead.

	cd addon/jsd && make clean && make JSD_DEBUG=1

enables the internal debug messages of JSD, which show the SDO transfers of the
drive configuration. This is the first thing to try, if a drive does not reach
the OPERATIONAL state.

If the bus does not become operational, the log shows which step failed. A
frequent cause is a drive parameter, which the drive rejects (see above), or a
loop period, which does not match the configured value.

The message

	slave[1] "..." vendor 0x9A product 0x900002 ...:
		Elmo product code is not known to JSD

means that the drive belongs to a supported family, but reports a product code,
which is not in the hard coded list of JSD, so it is not driven. If you are sure
about the family of your drive, build the library with the code:

	cd addon/jsd && make clean && make ELMO_EPD_PRODUCT_CODE=0x00900002

for a Platinum drive, or with ELMO_EGD_PRODUCT_CODE for a Gold drive, then
rebuild this sample. Please read the section "UNKNOWN PRODUCT CODES" in
addon/jsd/README first: it describes what to check on the drive afterwards,
before you arm it.

jsd_init() also logs the mapped process data sizes of every slave and compares
them with the sizes, which the driver expects. A mismatch fails the bus start
with a message instead of tripping an assert in the cyclic path later.


LIMITATIONS

The Circle scheduler is cooperative and non-preemptive, so the cycle can be
delayed by another task, which does not yield. The dashboard shows the number
of missed deadlines and the longest measured cycle period, so this is visible
rather than silent. There is no synchronization of the system time of the
Raspberry Pi to the distributed clock of the EtherCAT network.

This sample has never been tested against real Elmo hardware.
